  "rows": [
    {
      "consumer_paths": [
        "src/ocm/learning/methods.py"
      ],
      "heading": "M1: finite completeness and bounded convergence",
      "id": "M1",
      "parent_status": "PARENT_SUFFICIENT",
      "reopen_conditions": [
        "source_or_runtime_change",
        "source_revocation",
        "counterexample",
        "scope_or_assumption_change"
      ],
      "source_scope": "For `p` primitive instructions and maximum length `L`, the candidate universe has\n`N = sum(p**i for i=0..L)` words, including the empty program. All instructions\nand the checker terminate on the registered domain. Exhaustively evaluating the\nuniverse therefore decides existence **in this grammar and bound**.\n\nLet a correct program have primitive enumeration rank `r`, starting at 1. An\nalternating guided/primitive schedule reaches the first `r` primitive candidates\nwithin `2r` slots. Rejecting duplicate guided words does not remove the primitive\nslot. Thus any solution found by the primitive parent in `r` slots is found in\nat most `2r` slots, provided that budget is available. This is a slot bound, not\na bound on arbitrary-precision arithmetic time or memory. A slot includes failed,\nduplicate and over-length guided expansions; those costs cannot disappear.\n\nFinite budget exhaustion says nothing about longer programs. If the grammar is\nextended to every finite length and each candidate checker is total, fairness\ngives eventual discovery of an existing finite solution. It gives neither a\nuniform finite bound nor a terminating procedure for every unsatisfiable problem.\nThe executable OCM reference deliberately bounds length at 8 and slots at 200,000.",
      "source_status": "PROVED_SCOPE_LIMITED"
    },
    {
      "consumer_paths": [
        "src/ocm/learning/methods.py"
      ],
      "heading": "M2: examples, counterexamples and mathematical solutions",
      "id": "M2",
      "parent_status": "PARENT_SUFFICIENT",
      "reopen_conditions": [
        "source_or_runtime_change",
        "source_revocation",
        "counterexample",
        "scope_or_assumption_change"
      ],
      "source_scope": "Numeric evaluation and symbolic coefficient propagation are separate\nimplementations. Each primitive preserves polynomial semantics by the ordinary\naddition, scaling and convolution identities. Structural induction on program\nlength proves that coefficient propagation represents the program on every\nrational input. Equality of canonical coefficient tuples is therefore sufficient\nfor the requested polynomial identity, beyond the finitely sampled examples.\n\nIf a candidate fits the examples but has unequal coefficients, the difference\nis a nonzero degree-`d` polynomial. It cannot vanish at all of `0,...,d`, so a\ncounterexample exists among those `d+1` points. The example set can eliminate the\ncandidate without turning example agreement into a proof.\n\nThe proof obligation is confined to this total arithmetic language. Arbitrary\nPython callbacks, general theorem proving and Lean kernel integration do not\ninherit it. Host code is never synthesized or executed by this reference.",
      "source_status": "PROVED_SCOPE_LIMITED"
    },
    {
      "consumer_paths": [
        "src/ocm/learning/methods.py"
      ],
      "heading": "M3: method learning and evidence lifecycle",
      "id": "M3",
      "parent_status": "PARENT_SUFFICIENT",
      "reopen_conditions": [
        "source_or_runtime_change",
        "source_revocation",
        "counterexample",
        "scope_or_assumption_change"
      ],
      "source_scope": "Fragment support counts distinct specification hashes, not task labels or copied\ntraces. Only independently rechecked training solutions contribute. Holdout\nspecification hashes must be nonempty, distinct and disjoint from training.\nOne fixed proposal is tested; this holdout must not be reused for adaptive tuning\nand then described as an independent test.\n\nAcceptance means all declared holdout tasks are solved, every measured slot count\nis no worse than the primitive parent, and at least one is better. It proves a\nfinite measured statement only. Two selected examples cannot support a population\nspeedup, architecture superiority or novelty claim.\n\nThe runtime stores generator data and support links, rather than executable host\nobjects. Its warrant is the conjunction of training proof support and holdout\ncomparison support. If an essential support is revoked, the generator cannot be\nloaded after restart. The old program, outcomes and history remain addressable.\nReinstatement and newly acquired evidence remain distinct operations.",
      "source_status": "PROVED_SCOPE_LIMITED"
    },
    {
      "consumer_paths": [
        "src/ocm/science/finite_identification.py"
      ],
      "heading": "M4: convergence of experimental identification",
      "id": "M4",
      "parent_status": "PARENT_SUFFICIENT",
      "reopen_conditions": [
        "source_or_runtime_change",
        "source_revocation",
        "counterexample",
        "scope_or_assumption_change"
      ],
      "source_scope": "Freeze a finite deterministic model class `H`, a finite query family `Q`, and a\ncomplete prediction `h(q)` for each pair. Assume the true model belongs to `H`,\nobservations are accurate, and every surviving distinct pair is separated by a\nquery. Choose a query minimizing the largest resulting outcome partition.\n\nThe true model remains in the version space. Every non-singleton version space\nhas a separating query, whose observed partition removes at least one model.\nConsequently identification terminates within `|H|-1` observations. No balance\nassumption is needed. A logarithmic worst-case bound requires an additional\nbalanced-splitting assumption and is not implied by minimax selection alone.\n\nIdentical prediction rows remain observationally equivalent. An empty version\nspace means the class or the observations must be revised. Revocation removes\nconstraints and can expand the version space, reopening experiment selection.\nModel-class identity is immutable; expanding the class creates a new contract.\nNo claim here covers noise, missing models, uncontrolled confounding, measurement\nerror, nonstationarity, or empirical truth outside the registered experiments.",
      "source_status": "PROVED_SCOPE_LIMITED"
    }
  ],
  "schema": "ocm.theory-runtime-engineering.v2"
